cxd4.","Black was threatening …cxd4, winning a pawn. Bh4 takes care of it.","Black answers 16…Nc7, opening the line of the bishop on c8 to the h3 pawn.","Engine line: 16…Nc7 17.Bh7+ Kf8 18.Rad1 cxd4 19.exd4 Be7 (+2.25)."]},{"ply":41,"name":"21.Nxd5","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"21.Nxd5!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the knight on d5 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: Black's best reply is 21…Bh4 22.Qe3 Be6 23.Be4 instead (the engine rates it +0.22).","Takes the pawn on d5.","21.Nxd5 wins a pawn and threatens Bh7+, winning the queen for 6 points of material, picking it up with 22.Nxf7.","Takes the d5 pawn and wins a pawn once the captures settle.","It threatens Bh7+, winning the queen for 6 points of material, picking it up with 22.Nxf7, so Black has to spend the next move on it."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"21.Nxd5!! gives up the knight on e5 to stay in the game: after it White holds the balance (+0.22); anything else leaves White a little worse (−1.40)."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"The knight on e5 is attacked by the knight on c6, the bishop on f6 and the rook on e8, and 21.Nxd5 doesn't save it. That's a knight on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the d5 pawn first. Now Black can take only one of the two: the knight on d5 or the knight on e5."},{"title":"If Black takes the knight","text":"21…Qxd5 is met by 22.Bh7+. Then 23.Rxd5 takes the queen on d5, and 24.Rc5 adds pressure on the knight on c6. White ends up a piece and a pawn ahead: White is clearly better (+2.46)."},{"title":"If Black takes the knight","text":"21…Nxe5 is met by 22.fxe5, taking the knight on e5 and threatening Nxf6+, with a dangerous check. Then 23.Nb6 threatens Bh7+, winning the queen for 8 points of material, and 24.Bc4 threatens Rxd8+, winning the queen for a rook. White ends up a piece and a pawn ahead: White is a little better (+1.34)."},{"title":"Black's best try","text":"Black's best is to leave the knight alone and play 21…Bh4. White answers 22.Qe3, and the queen on f2 gets away, threatening Nb6, winning the queen for 8 points of material. Then 24.Nb4 threatens Nbxc6, winning the queen for a rook, and 25.Bxc6 takes the knight on c6. White ends up two pawns ahead: White holds the balance (+0.22)."},{"title":"Why not save the knight first?","text":"Saving it with 21.Nf3, the best way to do that, is clearly weaker (−1.40 against +0.22). Black answers 21…Bxc3, taking the knight on c3. That would leave White a little worse."}]},{"ply":46,"name":"23…Bxe5","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"23…Bxe5 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 50% to 35%.","points":["White answers 24.Nxa8, which takes the rook on a8.","After Nxa8, White wins 2 points of material within 3 moves.","Better was 23…Bh4 (0.00), which attacks the queen."],"better":"23…Bh4 threatens …Bxf2+, winning the queen, and gets the bishop out of the way of exf6. Engine line: 24.Qc5 Be7 25.Bh7+ Kh8 26.Qa5 Qc7 (0.00)."},{"ply":49,"name":"25.Be4","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"25.Be4 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 62% to 51%.","points":["Black answers 25…Bxa2, which takes the pawn on a2.","After Bxa2, Black wins 1 point of material within 2 moves.","Better was 25.b3 (+1.33), which protects the a2 pawn, which was hanging."],"better":"25.b3 protects the a2 pawn. Engine line: 25…g6 26.Be4 Qc8 27.Qe3 Bb8 28.Qb6 (+1.33)."},{"ply":50,"name":"25…Qc8","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"25…Qc8 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 49% to 36%.","points":["White answers 26.b3, which takes c4 away from the Black queen on c8.","Better was 25…Bxa2 (+0.09), which takes the pawn on a2."],"better":"25…Bxa2 wins a pawn. Engine line: 26.Kh1 Be6 27.Qb6 Qb8 28.Bxb7 a5 (+0.09)."},{"ply":108,"name":"54…Kh5","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"54…Kh5 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 49% to 8%.","points":["White answers 55.Rxf4, which takes the bishop on f4.","After Rxf4, White wins 3 points of material within 2 moves.","Better was 54…Be5 (+0.12)."],"better":"54…Be5 prepares …Bd6 and …Kf7. Engine line: 55.Rc4 Bd6 56.Kc3 Kf7 57.Kb3 Ke6 (+0.12)."},{"ply":109,"name":"55.Rxf4","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"55.Rxf4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the bishop on f4.","The next best move, 55.Ke2, would drop White's winning chances from 92% to 53% (+0.29).","55.Rxf4 wins a bishop.","Takes the bishop on f4 and wins a bishop once the captures settle.","It prepares Rc4: the rook clears the way.","Black answers 55…Rb8."]}],"opening":{"name":"Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Positional Variation","eco":"D35","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 9…h6: 18 moves in all, 9 by White and 9 by Black.","Theory runs out after 9…h6: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}